  "response": "## Most likely cause: `daysSince` thresholds computed from the wall clock\n\n`src/billing.ts:92` (and the identical copy at `src/billing.ts:273`):\n\n```ts\nconst daysSince = Math.floor((Date.now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));\nif (user.plan !== \"free\") {\n  if (daysSince > 365)      discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.1);\n  else if (daysSince > 180) discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.05);\n  else if (daysSince > 90)  discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.02);\n}\n```\n\nA test fixture whose `createdAt` is \"90 days ago\" (or 180, or 365) lands on a threshold that `Math.floor` resolves differently depending on what time of day the suite runs, and differently again across a DST transition — 89 vs 90 flips `discountCents`, which flows into `subtotalCents → taxCents → totalCents`. That is exactly the shape of a date-boundary flake: green all morning, red after the clock crosses a day boundary or after CI moves to a machine in another zone.\n\n**Caveat on the scan output:** `crime_00012` classifies all 7 clock reads in `billing.ts` as \"none decide a branch — all 7 record or render the reading\", and scores it medium/0.34 agent risk on that basis. That is wrong for line 92. The read doesn't sit *in* the comparison; it's laundered through `daysSince` one line earlier and then compared three times. The detector's data-flow only looks one hop. Treat `crime_00012` as if it carried `direct_date`'s branch-deciding phrasing — the same phrasing `crime_00001` uses for `src/date.ts:4`, where it scored high.\n\n## Secondary suspects, in order\n\n| Finding | Location | Why it could produce the same symptom |\n|---|---|---|\n| `dst_naive_arithmetic` (`crime_00018`) | `date.ts:52` — `nowMs + 86400000` | \"Tomorrow\" is 23h or 25h on DST-transition days. Any billing test that builds a due date this way drifts by an hour, which is enough to cross midnight. |\n| `mixed_utc_local_methods` (`crime_00009`) | `date.ts:41` — `d.getUTCFullYear() + \"-\" + d.getMonth() + \"-\" + d.getDate()` | UTC year read against local month/day. For hours either side of UTC midnight the label is internally inconsistent. (It also emits a 0-indexed month and no zero-padding — separate bug, same line.) |\n| `timezone_unsafe_parse` (`crime_00011`) | `date.ts:29,33` | `\"2026-12-20\"` parses as **UTC** midnight; `\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\"` parses as **local**. Comparing the two mixes time domains — a period boundary computed from these two constants shifts by the runner's offset. |\n| `direct_date` (`crime_00001`) | `date.ts:4` — `isExpired` | Genuinely branch-deciding on `Date.now()`, and the scan agrees (high, 0.9). If the failing assertion is about expiry rather than money, start here instead. |\n\n## What makes it hard to pin, per the same scan\n\n- `crime_00015`: `generateInvoice` is 204 lines (`billing.ts:50–253`) with no seam — there's no parameter you can pass a fake clock through, so nobody added one.\n- `crime_00025` / `crime_00031`: both existing tests in `billing.test.ts` assert nothing real (`renderEnterprisePlan()` with zero expects; `expect(createInvoice()).toBeTruthy()` against a local stub). Whatever test is failing, these two aren't protecting the invoice path — don't read their passing as evidence.\n- `crime_00033`: `Invoice.createdAt` exists in `billing.ts:28` but is absent from the `Invoice` in `api/state.ts:9`. If the test builds its fixture against the `api/state` shape, `createdAt` is `undefined` → `Date.now() - undefined` → `NaN` → `daysSince` is `NaN` → every threshold is false. That's a *hard* fail rather than an intermittent one, so it's a lower-probability match for \"flaky\" — but it's the same field, worth ruling out first because it's a one-minute check.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nInject a clock and make the day math calendar-aware. Two changes, both small.\n\n**1. Thread a clock through the boundary** (`crime_00012`'s `inject_clock` action):\n\n```ts\nexport interface Clock { now(): number }\nconst systemClock: Clock = { now: () => Date.now() };\n\nexport function generateInvoice(\n  user: User,\n  items: InvoiceLine[],\n  paymentMethod?: string,\n  clock: Clock = systemClock,\n): Invoice {\n  const createdAt = clock.now();\n  // ...\n  const discountCents = loyaltyDiscountCents(subtotal, user, createdAt);\n```\n\nDefault argument keeps every existing caller working; tests pass `{ now: () => FIXED }`.\n\n**2. Extract the tiering as a pure function, and count calendar days rather than dividing milliseconds:**\n\n```ts\nconst utcDayNumber = (ms: number) => Math.floor(ms / 86_400_000); // UTC days since epoch\n\nexport function loyaltyDiscountCents(subtotalCents: number, user: User, nowMs: number): number {\n  if (user.plan === \"free\") return 0;\n  const daysSince = utcDayNumber(nowMs) - utcDayNumber(user.createdAt);\n  if (daysSince > 365) return Math.round(subtotalCents * 0.1);\n  if (daysSince > 180) return Math.round(subtotalCents * 0.05);\n  if (daysSince > 90)  return Math.round(subtotalCents * 0.02);\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\nCounting whole UTC days between two instants — rather than dividing the elapsed interval — removes the time-of-day dependence entirely: two timestamps on the same UTC date always differ by 0, regardless of when the suite runs. `recommendUpgrade` (`billing.ts:273`) has the same duplicated computation and should call the same helper.\n\nIf your billing periods are anchored to a customer's local timezone rather than UTC, `utcDayNumber` is the wrong primitive — use `Temporal.ZonedDateTime.from(...).since(..., { largestUnit: \"day\" })` or `date-fns-tz`'s `differenceInCalendarDays` in the customer's zone, which is what `crime_00018` is pointing at. Either way the rule is the same: never derive a day count by dividing a millisecond delta.\n\n**3. Then write the test that was missing** — the boundary is now expressible:\n\n```ts\nconst DAY = 86_400_000;\nconst NOW = Date.parse(\"2026-06-15T00:00:00Z\");\nconst clock = { now: () => NOW };\n\nit.each([[89, 0], [90, 0], [91, 0.02]])(\n  \"day %i → %f discount tier\",\n  (days, rate) => {\n    const user = { ...proUser, createdAt: NOW - days * DAY };\n    expect(loyaltyDiscountCents(10_000, user, clock.now())).toBe(Math.round(10_000 * rate));\n  },\n);\n```\n\nRun it under `TZ=UTC`, `TZ=America/New_York`, and `TZ=Pacific/Auckland` (southern-hemisphere DST, opposite phase) — if all three agree, the boundary is genuinely pinned rather than accidentally passing in your local zone.\n\n---\n\nOne note on the invocation: you scanned `src/billing.ts`, but the report covers 21 files across the whole fixture (`coverage.files_total: 21`, findings in `src/date.ts`, `src/petty.ts`, `docs/`, `src/assets/`). That worked in your favour here — the `date.ts` temporal findings are the ones that matter and a strict path filter would have hidden them — but the path argument didn't scope the scan the way the command reads like it should.",
